! mcp_bootstrap_p147.strap -- "ERROR CONTROL", printed page 147.
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.154.  Header read, not assumed:
! "TIME CLOCK 00054041  PUNID 11011MCP   CARD ID HA000001
!  DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER 147".
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FORTY-EIGHT printed lines, on forty-five cards (HA000001 ..
! HA000045 -- a NEW card series; printed p.144 was FB).  Nothing
! fell off either end: line 1 is the first asterisk of the heading
! box and line 48 is the last row with ink on the page, with the
! bottom third of the column blank below it.
!
!   lines 1-3   the asterisk box around "***** ERROR CONTROL *****"
!   line  4     "SLC,$", printed at 217371.70
!   lines 5-25  twenty-one instructions
!   line 26     "CNOP" -- which compiles NOTHING here (see below)
!   lines 27-28 two entry-mode (AX)/(A*) DD cards, the operator
!               message "1YOUR PROGRAM WAS ENDED BECAUSE OF A TYPE
!               XX ERROR"
!   lines 29-48 twenty more instructions, including a three-half-
!               word VF/CF/VF parameter block for S A8IQS
!
! Two routines share the page.  S DISP (line 5) is MCP's error
! dispatcher: it tests the S L bit ("LEVEL OF INSTRUCTION",
! printed p.103 line 14) and branches to MCPERR if the error is
! MCP's own; otherwise it decides from the error code in $14
! whether the problem is a special-assignment error (S ASNER), an
! I-O error (S IOERR) or neither, sets the MCP bounds and mask,
! moves the PP's lower registers to the permanent buffer, primes
! abnormal end-of-job through S PRIME with the parameter D ABEOJ,
! plants the error code in the spool message M PPBAD and goes to
! the spool printer through Z SPLPR.  MCPERR (line 29) is the
! other end: MCP itself has failed, so it saves 14 and 15, puts
! the code into MCPBAD (a message on the NEXT page), converts it
! to characters through S A8IQS, writes it on the console channel
! and then branches to itself -- "-MCP CANNOT CONTINUE".
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE JOIN, BACKWARD AND FORWARD
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! BACKWARD.  Line 4 prints "SLC,$" at 217371.70.  An SLC emits
! nothing, so that printed location IS the location counter
! arriving from printed p.146, free and in IBM's own hand: p.146
! must end at 217371.70 -- word 217371, bit 0o70 = 56 decimal.
! The ".70" is not a misread: it is printed with a closed 7 and
! a closed 0 at 900 dpi and the digit pattern is unlike any
! half-word offset on the page.  A page ending at bit 56 of a
! word means p.146's last card is a VFL/character datum, not an
! instruction.  >>> PREDICTION FOR p.146's TRANSCRIBER: your last
! row ends at 217371.70, i.e. 56 bits into word 217371. <<<
!
! This deck restates the origin hard as "SLC,(8)217371.70" -- the
! PRINTED value, not 217372.00 -- and lets strap_asm align line
! 5's full word up to 217372.00 by itself.  A hard SLC cannot
! disagree with an arriving counter (transcription_workflow.md,
! "WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE" 2), so the join has to be compared
! by hand when p.146 lands; the number to compare against is
! written above and in the line-4 listing row below.
!
! FORWARD, AND IT IS ALREADY CLOSED.  decks/mcp_bootstrap_p119.
! strap carries "SDISP SYN,(8)217372.0", read off printed p.119
! lines 43 and 46, which print "B,S DISP" compiling 217372.10.
! That is this page's line 5, pinned from a page transcribed
! before it and by a different reader.  Line 5 of this page lands
! at 217372.00 from the arriving counter alone.  The two agree.
!
! The page's last row is line 48 at 217432.00, a half word, so
! printed p.148 begins at 217432.40.  Independently: the volume
! OCR of p.148 carries "217435.CC 217451.C2 30 LV,1,ZEC75S" and
! "217437.0C ... MCCW1 CW,0" and "217440.0C -217443.00+ ...
! MMCPCW CW,MCPBAD,5.0" and "217443.00 ... MCPBAD DD(BU),(16)
! FFFF...", so p.148 runs 217432.40 .. 217446.70 at least.
! >>> PREDICTION FOR p.148's TRANSCRIBER: your first row is at
! 217432.40, and your page defines MCCW1 at 217437.00, MMCPCW at
! 217440.00 (a CW whose address field is 217443.00) and MCPBAD at
! 217443.00. <<<
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPHS -- WHAT WAS SETTLED AND ON WHAT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This page drops ink on round glyphs constantly: its own column
! heading prints "LCCATICN" and "BINARY CUTPUT", and locations
! print "217373.CC" for .00.  Every round glyph in a LOCATION or
! in an OCTAL binary field is a 0 with certainty.  The four that
! are NOT in an octal field were each settled on something else.
!
! (1) LINE 6's BYTE: "C4" IS "04".  Line 6 prints
!         C04C02.35 C4      KVI,$14,SASNER
!     and line 8, three rows down and the SAME STATEMENT SHAPE,
!     prints 003405.35 04 with four closed zeros.  Line 6's own
!     octal field carries THREE impossible C's ("C04C02"), so
!     that row is demonstrably dropping ink.  Off the page: the
!     volume OCR has sixteen other KVI rows on fifteen other
!     printed pages and every one of them is byte 04.  The
!     assembler agrees: KVI,$14,SASNER compiles 004002.35 04.
!
! (2) LINE 29's BYTE: "AC" IS "A0", BY THE i2 RULE AND BY LINE 46.
!     Line 29 prints "COOC36.CC 80  217441.C4 AC" -- five
!     impossible C's in octal fields in one row.  The low nibble
!     of a VFL/transmit full word is i2, the index on operand 2,
!     and line 29's statement "TI,2,$14,MMCPCW+1.0" has no "(I)"
!     on either operand, so i2 = 0.  Line 46 is the same TI with
!     the operands swapped, also unindexed, and prints a clean
!     A0 at 900 dpi.  Both compile A0.
!
! (3) "(SECP)" IS "(SEOP)", AND THE ASSEMBLER ELIMINATES THE
!     OTHER READING.  Lines 37 and 45 print "REL(SECP)" and
!     "W(SECP)".  SECP is not an I-O modifier at all; SEOP
!     (Suppress End-Of-Operation) is, and MCP printed pp.3-4
!     already print "REL(SEOP),0.0($15)".  Measured:
!         REL(SEOP),.0($14)   000000.00 8E 000000.33 00 = PRINTED
!         REL(SECP),.0($14)   "unrecognized I-O mnemonic/modifier
!                              combination"
!
! (4) "Z,$INC" IS "Z,$IND" (line 10).  $INC is not a system
!     symbol; $IND is index-and-mask word 0o13, and "Z,$IND"
!     compiles the printed 000013.22 00.  printed p.144 settled
!     the same glyph the same way on its own lines 17/28.
!
! (5) "SA8IQS" (line 39) -- B, NOT 8, SETTLED BY THE CALLING
!     SEQUENCE AND NOT BY INK.  Printed p.77 lines 18-22 are:
!         LVI,$15,TP6A  /  BD,SA8IQS  /  TP6A VF,TP5IN  /  CF,8
!         /  VF,TP5OUT
!     and this page's lines 38-42 are:
!         LVI,$15,$+1.0 /  B,SA8IQS   /  VF,MCPBAD+3.56 / CF,2.0
!         /  VF,MCPBAD+3.56
!     -- the identical five-card idiom (set $15 to the parameter
!     block, branch to the 8-bit conversion routine, FWA-in /
!     count / FWA-out), from a landed deck on a different page.
!     See the EXTERNALS note for the address, which is NOT p.77's.
!
! WHAT ONLY THE INK SAYS, AND SO IS NOT CLAIMED AS CHECKED:
! lines 27 and 28 print no BINARY OUTPUT at all (an entry-mode DD
! prints nothing in that column on this listing), so the thirty-
! two and twenty-four CHARACTERS of the message rest on reading
! the statement column, and a wrong LETTER inside either message
! is invisible to every check on this page, forever.
!
! BE EXACT ABOUT WHAT THE COUNTER CHECKS, BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS
! CLAIM IS TOO STRONG.  Line 29's !CHECK at 217415.00 verifies
! the SUM of the two data, 32 + 24 = 56 characters = 448 bits =
! seven words, and NOT the split.  Measured, moving one blank
! from line 28's trailer into line 27's:
!     32 / 24 characters   line 29 lands at 217415.00
!     33 / 23 characters   line 29 lands at 217415.00
! -- strap_asm does not pad an entry-mode DD up to a word, so the
! two land identically.  What pins the SPLIT is line 28's own
! PRINTED LOCATION, 217412.00: measured the same way, the 33/23
! variant starts the second datum one character late (the text at
! 217412.00 reads " OF A TYPE" instead of "OF A TYPE "), i.e. at
! 217412.10, which the page does not print.  That comparison is
! BY HAND, because line 28 prints no binary and carries no label,
! so no !SYM or !CHECK can express it.  It is recorded here
! rather than claimed by a passing row.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! LINE 26's CNOP COMPILES NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE POINT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Line 26 prints "CNOP" with a BLANK location and a BLANK binary
! column.  Line 25 is a half word at 217405.40, so the counter
! already stands at 217406.00 and the CNOP has nothing to round.
! Contrast printed p.84 line 8 and printed p.133 line 1, where
! the counter stands on an odd half word and the CNOP prints its
! own location and a 000000.30 00 no-op half word.  So a blank
! CNOP row is the operation working, not a crop landing short.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE BLANK-OPERATION CARDS (lines 19, 24, 25)
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Three cards print a statement whose OPERATION FIELD IS EMPTY --
! ",DABEOJ", ",MPPBAD", ",7.0" -- the comma standing in the same
! column as the comma of "B,ZSPLPR" one row up.  Each compiles
! one half word: the operand's address in bits 0-23 and 0x80 in
! bits 24-31.  They are the in-line parameters of the SIC/branch
! pair immediately above them (lines 17-18 call S PRIME with
! D ABEOJ; lines 22-23 call Z SPLPR with M PPBAD and 7.0), which
! is how a Stretch subroutine takes arguments: the callee reads
! them through the IC that SIC saved.
!
! strap_asm already accepts a blank operation and already
! compiles exactly this, which is why all three rows are checked
! here rather than carried unchecked.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE HEADING-ROW "LOCATION" FIELD IS BLANK, AND WHY -- A RULE
! THAT docs/transcription_workflow.md CURRENTLY CALLS UNEXPLAINED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This page prints the word "LOCATION" at the right-hand end of
! the column-heading line with NOTHING after it (read at 600 dpi;
! no digits, only the dashed rule).  So there is no independent
! witness of the origin from that field, and none is invented
! here.
!
! But the pattern is now explicable.  Measured across every page
! the file records: pp.83, 144, 145 and 147 print the field
! BLANK, and pp.95, 120, 125, 133, 136, 138, 139, 141, 142 and
! 143 print a number.  Rendering line 1 of each:
!   * on all four BLANK pages, LINE 1 IS A CARD THAT COMPILES
!     NOTHING and has no LOCATION of its own -- p.147 lines 1-3
!     are the asterisk box, p.145 lines 1-4 are the asterisk box,
!     p.83 lines 1-5 are comment cards;
!   * on all ten NUMBERED pages, line 1 carries a location (p.136
!     "SIC,G KONEC" at 216661.00, p.138 at 217056.40, p.139 at
!     217116.00, p.142 at 217245.00, p.133's CNOP at 216565.40,
!     p.143 at 217310.40, p.120's SIC, p.125's LC, p.95's BAE).
! The field is therefore LINE 1's location, not the page's
! origin, and it is blank exactly when line 1 has none.  This
! page is itself a check on that: its first LOCATED line is line
! 4 at 217371.70 and the heading does NOT print 217371.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! LABELS PRINTED WITH BLANKS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! "S DISP" (5), "Z EC99" (36), "Z EC 75" (48) print with blanks
! and are written closed up, as every deck since p.2 does.
! SDISP is already spelled closed up by decks/mcp_bootstrap_p119
! .strap and by the MCP System Analysis Guide's prose ("SDISP
! Error; code in $14"), which is the independent witness.
! "S IO ERR" (line 32) and "SIOERR" (line 8) are the SAME SYMBOL
! printed two ways on one page -- both compile 003405.35 04 --
! which is the page's own demonstration that the blanks are not
! part of the name.  Likewise "S REJ" (34).
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SECOND ASTERISK
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Every line number carries an asterisk ("6*", "48*"); that is
! the ordinary column.  THREE lines carry a SECOND asterisk after
! the LOCATION: line 4 (217371.70, the SLC), line 24 (217405.00,
! ",MPPBAD") and line 34 (217420.40, "KVI,14,S REJ").  This is
! the unexplained marker of docs/listing_typography.md 12.  What
! this page adds, in both directions:
!
! WHAT DIES: the three marked rows have nothing in common -- an
! SLC that emits nothing, a blank-operation half word, and an
! ordinary KVI -- and lines 19 and 25, the other two blank-
! operation cards, are NOT marked, so "the mark follows the
! blank-operation card" is refuted on this page alone.
!
! WHAT SURVIVES, one-directional and cheap to falsify: EVERY
! PRINTED "SLC,$" ROW SEEN SO FAR CARRIES THE MARK.  Three for
! three -- printed p.144 line 5 (217326.00 *), printed p.145
! line 5 (217347.40 *, rendered for this note) and this page's
! line 4 (217371.70 *).  The converse is false (lines 24 and 34
! are marked and are not SLCs), so this predicts only that the
! next page's "SLC,$" will be marked, and one crop refutes it.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS -- each pinned by a card on a DIFFERENT page
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This page's own rows are OCR lines ~7660-7708 of
! docs/ocr/MCP_Mar63_001-249.txt; every witness cited below is at
! a different OCR line and a different printed page, so none of
! them is this page reading itself.
!
! S COM RG 215001.00  p.103 line 8 "S COM RG XW,(8)520000."
SCOMRG  SYN, (8)215001.0
! S L      215001.04  p.103 line 14 DEFINES it, "S L SYN(BU,1),
!                     S COM RG+0.04" -LEVEL OF INSTRUCTION.  Line
!                     5 here prints 215001.04 as a full 24-bit
!                     BIT address, so the row pins it to the bit.
SL      SYN(BU,1), SCOMRG+0.04
! S ASNER  004002.00  p.107 line 36 DEFINES it, "S ASNER SYN,(16)
!                     0802.0" -82 SPECIAL ASSIGNMENT; hex 0802 is
!                     octal 4002.  (decks/mcp_bootstrap_p107
!                     carries it at exactly this value.)
SASNER  SYN, (8)4002.0
! S IOERR  003405.00  p.107 line 29 DEFINES it, "S IOERR SYN,(16)
!                     0705.0" -75 MCP SETUP I/O ERROR.
SIOERR  SYN, (8)3405.0
! S REJ    003406.00  p.107 line 30 DEFINES it, "S REJ SYN,(16)
!                     0706.0" -76 I/O OPERATION REJECTED.
SREJ    SYN, (8)3406.0
! S BNDRS  215224.00  p.116 line 7 DEFINES it, "S BNDRS VF,33.0".
SBNDRS  SYN, (8)215224.0
! S LRMCP  215362.00  p.116 line 26 DEFINES it, "S LRMCP DRZ(BU,
!                     64),29", and p.116 line 27 prints "SLC,
!                     SLRMCP+9.0" resolving to 215373.00 -- which
!                     is the very word line 13 here prints.  That
!                     is the arithmetic checked on two pages.
SLRMCP  SYN, (8)215362.0
! S T LR   215230.00  p.116 line 13 "S T LR DRZ(BU,64),29";
!                     carried at this value by p.144's deck.
STLR    SYN, (8)215230.0
! S LRBU   215324.00  printed p.133 line 20 "YMSF TI1,16,STLR,
!                     SLRBU" (cited by decks/mcp_bootstrap_p114
!                     and p.115) -- the same two-operand pair as
!                     line 16 here.
SLRBU   SYN, (8)215324.0
! S PRIMR  215571.00  pp.132 and 193 print "SIC,SPRIMR" against
!                     215571.00 80; carried by p.103's deck.
SPRIMR  SYN, (8)215571.0
! S PRIME  215570.00  pp.132 and 193 print "B,SPRIME" against
!                     215570.10; carried by p.103's deck.  BD
!                     adds .04, which is what line 18 prints.
SPRIME  SYN, (8)215570.0
! D ABEOJ  000104.00  p.105 line 39 DEFINES it, "D ABEOJ SYN,
!                     68.0" (68 decimal = 0o104).
DABEOJ  SYN, (8)104.0
! Z SPLP9  224052.00  printed p.132 line 47 prints 224052.00 80
!                     for "SIC,ZSPLP9"; carried by p.132's deck.
ZSPLP9  SYN, (8)224052.0
! Z SPLPR  224046.40  printed p.132 line 48 prints 224046.50 for
!                     "B,ZSPLPR"; 0o50 is 0o40 (the half word)
!                     plus B's own 0o10.  Carried by p.132.
ZSPLPR  SYN, (8)224046.40
! S YCOCH  215006.00  p.103 line 37 DEFINES it, "S YCOCH VF,(8)
!                     23.40" -CONSOLE CHANNEL NUMBER, at
!                     215006.00; line 36's comment here is the
!                     same English, "-CONSOLE CHANNEL NUMBER".
SYCOCH  SYN, (8)215006.0
! S A8IQS  230566.00  NOT p.77's S A8IQS.  p.77 line 19 branches
!                     to an S A8IQS at 002366.00 -- the IPL's own
!                     copy of the 8-bit conversion routine, in
!                     low storage -- and the MCP body has a
!                     second copy at 230566.00.  The address is
!                     pinned OFF THIS PAGE: the volume OCR around
!                     printed address 221747 carries a row
!                     "221747.00  230566.10 00  B,SA?IQS", the
!                     identical half word from a different page.
!                     >>> ONE NAME, TWO ADDRESSES, ON PURPOSE:
!                     decks/mcp_bootstrap_p77.strap SYNs this
!                     same name to (8)2366.0.  Nothing on THIS
!                     page is wrong -- line 39 prints 230566.10
!                     and p.77 line 19 prints 002366.04 -- and
!                     the corpus already carries 78 names bound
!                     to more than one address across the
!                     IPL/MCP split (Z FINIS and D FREE among
!                     them), so this is the existing pattern and
!                     not a new hazard.  check_symbol_spellings
!                     passes with this deck in place (1254
!                     definitions, no symbol spelled two ways).
!                     Recorded here so the merge is not
!                     surprised by it.
SA8IQS  SYN, (8)230566.0
! M CCW1   217437.00  printed p.148, the NEXT page: the volume OCR
!                     carries "217437.0C  CC00.00+ ...  MCCW1
!                     CW,0  -COPIED C[W]" -- its defining card.
!                     Line 44 here prints 217437.30 for
!                     "MCCW1+.24" (24 decimal = 0o30), so the
!                     arithmetic agrees with that definition.
MCCW1   SYN, (8)217437.0
! M MCPCW  217440.00  printed p.148: the OCR carries "217440.0C
!                     -217443.00+ ...  MMCPCW CW,MCPBAD,5.0
!                     -WRITE CW" -- its defining card, AND that
!                     card's own address field is 217443.00,
!                     which independently pins M CPBAD below.
MMCPCW  SYN, (8)217440.0
! M CPBAD  217443.00  printed p.148: the OCR carries "217443.00
!                     ...  MCPBAD DD(BU),(16)FFFF..." -- the
!                     defining card.  Two checks on this page
!                     agree: lines 40/42 print the VF value
!                     217446.70+ for "MCPBAD+3.56" (56 decimal =
!                     0o70) and line 31 prints 217446.70 for the
!                     same expression as an SF address.
!                     NOTE M CPBAD (external, 217443.00) and
!                     M PPBAD (this page's line 27, 217406.00)
!                     are TWO DIFFERENT SYMBOLS that differ in
!                     one letter.  They are not a drum confusion:
!                     line 21's "MPPBAD+5.16" prints 217413.20
!                     and line 31's "MCPBAD+3.56" prints
!                     217446.70, and only two distinct bases
!                     produce both.
MCPBAD  SYN, (8)217443.0
! >>> HOW FAR EACH EXTERNAL IS ACTUALLY PINNED BY *THIS* PAGE.
! transcription_workflow.md, "WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE" 1: an
! address back-derived from a printed branch or LVI is known only
! to the HALF WORD, so writing ".0" is a CONVENTION there and not
! a reading.  Split for this page:
!   PINNED TO THE BIT by a row here, because the row prints a
!   full 24-bit address: S L (line 5's BB1 A1), S T LR (line 16's
!   transmit A1), M CPBAD (line 31's SF A1, and again as a 24-bit
!   VF value on lines 40/42), M CCW1 (line 44's BB A1), M MCPCW
!   (line 46's TI A1), and -- less obviously -- D ABEOJ and
!   M PPBAD, because the blank-operation cards on lines 19 and 24
!   emit bits 18-23 as the offset field, so "000104.00" and
!   "217406.00" show the offset and not only the half word.
!   S ASNER, S IOERR and S REJ are immediates, printed in full.
!   S BNDRS and S LRMCP come through LX, whose A is a word
!   address, and p.116's defining cards pin them anyway.
!   PINNED ONLY TO THE HALF WORD by this page, rescued by a
!   defining card elsewhere: S PRIMR, S PRIME (p.103's deck),
!   Z SPLP9, Z SPLPR (p.132's deck), M CCW1/M MCPCW/M CPBAD
!   again from p.148's own cards, Z EC75S (p.148's LV row).
!   WEAKEST, AND SAID SO: S LRBU.  Its only witness is printed
!   p.133's "YMSF TI1,16,STLR,SLRBU", which is itself an
!   INSTRUCTION -- a transmit's A2 is nineteen bits, word plus
!   half-word select -- so S LRBU is pinned to the half word from
!   both sides and no defining card is known for it.  The ".0" in
!   its SYN below is convention.  Same for S A8IQS, whose only
!   witnesses are two branches.
!
! Z EC75S  217451.00  printed p.148: the OCR carries "217435.CC
!                     217451.C2 30  LV,1,ZEC75S" -- the same
!                     symbol in an LV on a DIFFERENT page, whose
!                     0o02 is LV's J=1 operation bits where line
!                     48's 0o03 is SV's.  Same word, two
!                     operations, two pages.
ZEC75S  SYN, (8)217451.0

! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE ORIGIN.  The page prints "SLC,$" (line 4) at 217371.70.
! Restated hard at the PRINTED value; strap_asm aligns line 5's
! full word up to 217372.00 on its own.  See THE JOIN.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)217371.70

! ---- S DISP: the error dispatcher --------------------------------
SDISP   BB1, SL, MCPERR              ! line  5  -MCP CONTAMINATION IF BRANCH
        KVI, $14, SASNER             ! line  6
        BXH, $+1.32                  ! line  7  (same card as 6)
        KVI, $14, SIOERR             ! line  8
        BZXL, MCPERR                 ! line  9  (same card as 8)
        Z, $IND                      ! line 10
        LX, 0, SBNDRS                ! line 11
        SX, 0, 3.0                   ! line 12  -SET MCP BOUNDS (same card as 11)
        LX, 0, SLRMCP+9.0            ! line 13
        SX, 0, $MASK                 ! line 14  -SET MCP MASK (same card as 13)
        LCI, 15, 30.0                ! line 15
        T, 15, STLR, SLRBU           ! line 16  -MOVE PP LOWER REGS. TO PERM. BUFFER
        SIC, SPRIMR                  ! line 17
        BD, SPRIME                   ! line 18  (same card as 17)
        , DABEOJ                     ! line 19  -PRIME ABNORMAL E O J
        SV, 14, $L                   ! line 20  -GET ERROR CODE
        SF(BU,16), MPPBAD+5.16, -18  ! line 21  -SET ERROR CODE IN SPOOL MESSAGE
        SIC, ZSPLP9                  ! line 22
        B, ZSPLPR                    ! line 23
        , MPPBAD                     ! line 24
        , 7.0                        ! line 25
        CNOP                         ! line 26  (compiles nothing; counter is already aligned)
! ---- the operator message ----------------------------------------
MPPBAD  (AX)DD(BU),1YOUR PROGRAM WAS ENDED BECAUSE X
        (A*)DD(BU),OF A TYPE XX ERROR      *
! ---- MCPERR: MCP's own failure -----------------------------------
MCPERR  TI, 2, $14, MMCPCW+1.0       ! line 29  -SAVE 14 AND 15
        SV, $14, $L                  ! line 30
        SF(BU,16), MCPBAD+3.56, -18  ! line 31  -ERROR CODE
        KVI, 14, SIOERR              ! line 32  (printed "S IO ERR")
        BXE, ZEC75                   ! line 33
        KVI, 14, SREJ                ! line 34  (printed "S REJ")
        BXE, ZEC75                   ! line 35
ZEC99   LV, 14, SYCOCH               ! line 36  -CONSOLE CHANNEL NUMBER
        REL(SEOP), .0($14)           ! line 37
        LVI, $15, $+1.0              ! line 38
        B, SA8IQS                    ! line 39
        VF, MCPBAD+3.56              ! line 40  -FWA IN
